  <dimension1_explanation>The AI correctly identified symmetry as the key tool and even employed digit-relabeling symmetry (which is part of the standard solution), but it could not see the problem's image and thus never discovered the crucial geometric configuration: the two known squares force digit 1 (and 2) into one of six specific positions in the third row, where column-swap symmetry gives each position probability 1/6. Without this 1/6 anchor, the AI guessed configurations and settled on 1/7 (a+b=8) instead of the correct 2/21 (a+b=23).</dimension1_explanation>
  <dimension2_turning_point_type>other</dimension2_turning_point_type>
  <dimension2_explanation>The standard solution's key turning point is a Sudoku-specific geometric symmetry argument: the known squares constrain the third row so that digit 1 must occupy one of six marked squares, and column-swap symmetries (swapping columns 4↔5, and swapping the 4-5-6 block with the 7-8-9 block) make all six equally likely, yielding P(?=1)=1/6. This is a combinatorial/positional symmetry argument, not a number-theoretic technique.</dimension2_explanation>
  <ai_direction_summary>The AI recognized symmetry as the right tool and extensively developed digit-relabeling symmetry arguments, but spent all its effort guessing the unknown image configuration and testing generic cases, never reaching the specific third-row six-square geometric insight.</ai_direction_summary>
  <standard_solution_key_technique>Combine positional column-swap symmetry (giving P(?=1)=P(?=2)=1/6 from the six candidate squares in the third row) with digit-relabeling symmetry (making the remaining seven digits equally likely at ?), yielding (1−1/6−1/6)/7 = 2/21.</standard_solution_key_technique>
  <confidence>high</confidence>
</analysis>
